  "numerical_failure": {
    "category": "correctness",
    "affected_api": "cvxpy.reductions.eliminate_pwl.canonicalizers.sum_largest_canon.sum_largest_canon",
    "observed_behavior": "DNLP warm-start initialization for sum_largest uses an incorrect threshold q (k-th smallest instead of k-th largest) and only initializes t for the top-k elements, yielding an infeasible warm start that violates feasibility constraints.",
    "expected_behavior": "Compute q as the minimum of the k largest elements (equivalently the k-th largest threshold) and initialize t for all elements as max(0, x - q), so that for all i: x_i <= t_i + q and t_i >= 0 hold.",
    "manifestation": "wrong_value",
    "trigger_conditions": [
      "When sum_largest_canon DNLP warm-start is used (x.value is not None).",
      "Any input where there exist 'middle' elements between the computed q and the top-k values, i.e., some elements with x_i > q (the discrepancy specifically violates feasibility for these middle elements).",
      "Using the previous (buggy) warm-start code path that computed q from k smallest and initialized t only on top-k indices."
    ],
    "input_output_examples": [
      {
        "input": "x.value contains values where there are 'middle' elements with x_i > q (q computed from the k-th smallest instead of k-th largest); warm-start DNLP initialization in sum_largest_canon",
        "buggy_output": "Warm-start values q.value computed from the k-th smallest element and t.value only nonzero for top-k elements, causing violations of the constraint x_i <= t_i + q for those middle elements.",
        "expected_output": "q.value computed as min(x.value[idx_of_largest]) (k-th largest threshold) and t.value initialized for all elements as max(0, x.value - q.value), ensuring x_i <= t_i + q and t_i >= 0 for all i.",
        "value_kind": "unknown",
        "source": "PR#3220 description and fix summary; diff shows warm-start logic change."
      }
    ],
    "oracle_clues": [
      "The LP epigraph form of sum_k_largest(x) requires constraint x_i <= t_i + q and t_i >= 0.",
      "Bug summary: q was set to max(k smallest) instead of min(k largest), and t was only initialized for top-k elements, making warm start infeasible for middle elements where x_i > q.",
      "Fix summary: use q = min(x[top-k]) and t = max(0, x - q) for all elements.",
      "Regression tests verify q and t computed correctly and that constraints x_i <= t_i + q and t_i >= 0 hold for all elements, including middle ones."
    ]
  },
  "numerical_characteristics": {
    "dtype": null,
    "precision": null,
    "magnitude": null,
    "tolerance": null,
    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "The LP epigraph form of `sum_k_largest(x)` has the constraint `x_i <= t_i + q`.",
      "Bug: `q` computed using the k-th smallest element instead of the k-th largest.",
      "Bug: `t` was only initialized for top-k elements, leaving \u201cmiddle\u201d elements without a feasible initialization.",
      "Consequently, for \u201cmiddle\u201d indices where `x_i > q`, the feasibility constraint `x_i <= t_i + q` is violated.",
      "Concrete test values: k=2; x.value = [1.0, 10.0, 6.0, 8.0, 3.0, 9.0]; the top-2 (largest) are {10.0, 9.0}; the middle elements 6.0 and 8.0 triggered the bug.",
      "Fix described: compute `q` as `min(x[top-k])` (k-th largest) and initialize `t` for all elements as `t = max(0, x - q)` so that `x_i <= t_i + q` and `t_i >= 0` hold for all elements, including the middle ones."
    ],
